WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.766
Three levels of arithmetic have produced variances. A verdict

2
00:00:04.866 --> 00:00:07.957
is not a variance. To get one you divide

3
00:00:08.057 --> 00:00:11.228
- and the question is what you divide by.

4
00:00:11.316 --> 00:00:14.693
Here is the gauge, drawn to scale. And here are the

5
00:00:14.793 --> 00:00:18.307
two things you might compare it to: the spread of the

6
00:00:18.407 --> 00:00:21.920
parts you measured, and the tolerance on the drawing.

7
00:00:22.016 --> 00:00:26.674
Same numerator. Two denominators. Two questions - can this gauge

8
00:00:26.774 --> 00:00:30.465
tell these parts apart, and can it decide whether a

9
00:00:30.565 --> 00:00:34.999
part conforms. Neither is wrong, and the standard prints both

10
00:00:35.099 --> 00:00:37.972
without saying which one you are asking.

11
00:00:39.766 --> 00:00:43.289
Watch what happens to each of them when the process

12
00:00:43.389 --> 00:00:46.770
gets better and the gauge does not change at all.

13
00:00:46.866 --> 00:00:52.574
The tolerance ratio is a flat line. It does not know the parts exist.

14
00:00:52.666 --> 00:00:56.987
The study ratio climbs, and it climbs steeply. Make the parts

15
00:00:57.087 --> 00:01:01.988
nearly identical and it approaches a hundred percent, because a gauge

16
00:01:02.088 --> 00:01:06.772
measuring parts that do not differ cannot tell them apart. Nothing

17
00:01:06.872 --> 00:01:10.830
about the instrument got worse. The question got harder.

18
00:01:12.616 --> 00:01:15.789
So put one gauge - this gauge, two point

19
00:01:15.889 --> 00:01:19.716
zero six microns - into two different factories.

20
00:01:19.816 --> 00:01:24.377
In the first, the parts barely vary and the drawing is generous.

21
00:01:24.477 --> 00:01:28.602
The gauge cannot tell the parts apart at all - fifty seven

22
00:01:28.702 --> 00:01:34.428
percent, reject - and it decides conformance comfortably, eight percent, accept.

23
00:01:34.516 --> 00:01:38.466
In the second, exactly the reverse. It sorts the parts

24
00:01:38.566 --> 00:01:42.816
easily and it cannot be trusted with the conformance call.

25
00:01:42.916 --> 00:01:46.952
One gauge. Two rows. Opposite verdicts off the same

26
00:01:47.052 --> 00:01:50.520
printed table, and neither row is a mistake.

27
00:01:51.316 --> 00:01:55.103
The standard prints a third number beside those two:

28
00:01:55.203 --> 00:01:58.989
the number of distinct categories. It is supposed to

29
00:01:59.089 --> 00:02:02.352
say how many groups the gauge can tell apart.

30
00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:06.591
Plot it against the study ratio and it is a curve. Not

31
00:02:06.691 --> 00:02:11.305
a cloud - a curve. Given the study ratio, ndc is determined,

32
00:02:11.405 --> 00:02:16.646
because it is that same ratio with the algebra rearranged. It cannot

33
00:02:16.746 --> 00:02:21.046
carry information the first number did not already have.

34
00:02:21.133 --> 00:02:26.117
Now put both of the standard's own gates on it. The rule says

35
00:02:26.217 --> 00:02:31.785
reject above thirty percent. The rule also says ndc must reach five.

36
00:02:31.883 --> 00:02:36.136
Those are not the same line. Ndc reaching five happens at

37
00:02:36.236 --> 00:02:41.329
twenty seven point one percent, nearly three points tighter than the

38
00:02:41.429 --> 00:02:45.759
thirty printed beside it. A gauge can satisfy one rule and

39
00:02:45.859 --> 00:02:49.807
fail the other, in the same table, on the same study.

40
00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:56.245
None of those three numbers is the thing you actually care about.

41
00:02:56.345 --> 00:03:00.747
A conformance decision compares a reading to a limit, so a good

42
00:03:00.847 --> 00:03:04.392
part can be rejected and a bad part can be shipped.

43
00:03:04.483 --> 00:03:08.080
The dashed lines are the truth's limits. The solid

44
00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:11.703
lines are where the gauge's reading crosses them.

45
00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:15.252
Five hundred parts, measured once each.

46
00:03:15.350 --> 00:03:20.207
The salmon points are out of tolerance and were accepted. The amber

47
00:03:20.307 --> 00:03:25.534
ones were inside and got scrapped. Twenty eight percent of the genuinely

48
00:03:25.634 --> 00:03:29.530
bad parts pass, on a centred process, with this gauge.

49
00:03:29.616 --> 00:03:34.715
Now move the process off centre without touching the gauge. Scrap goes

50
00:03:34.815 --> 00:03:38.353
from a tenth of a percent to five and a half. The

51
00:03:38.453 --> 00:03:43.552
gauge is identical, so every percentage from the first three levels is

52
00:03:43.652 --> 00:03:47.636
identical, and the risk has moved by a factor of forty.

53
00:03:47.733 --> 00:03:51.160
So a percentage is a proxy for a risk, and it does

54
00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:55.604
not know where your process is sitting. Which is the last thing

55
00:03:55.704 --> 00:04:00.613
we have been assuming without checking: that the gauge is merely noisy,

56
00:04:00.713 --> 00:04:05.057
and not wrong. Level five asks what happens when it reads high.

